/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Air-fuel ratio (A/F) is the mass of air divided by the mass of fuel entering the engine.
   - ṁair and ṁfuel are the air and fuel mass flows in kg/s; A/F is by mass and has no unit.
   - Air first passes the air filter, which stops dust that would grind the cylinder walls, rings and valve seats.
   - The throttle is a butterfly valve after the venturi, worked by the accelerator.
   - A simple carburettor has a float chamber, a fuel nozzle with the main jet at its foot, a venturi, the throttle and a choke.
   - Treat the air as incompressible across the small depression Δp.
   - ṁa = Cda At √(2 ρa Δp) ṁf = Cdf Af √(2 ρf (Δp − ρf g z)) At is the throat area and Af the jet area in m²; Cda, Cdf are discharge coefficients; ρa, ρf are the air and petrol densities in kg/m³; Δp is the depression in Pa; z is the tip height
   - If z is neglected, A/F = (CdaAt ÷ CdfAf) √(ρa ÷ ρf): two fixed areas give one mixture strength.
   - At small openings the idle and main circuits overlap; at normal part throttle the main circuit does the work.
*/
