/* book Worked example 2: Q: A simple carburettor must supply 7.2 kg/h of petrol at A/F = 15. Air is at 1.013 bar and 27 °C; the venturi depression is 0.065 bar. The nozzle tip is 5 mm above the float level. Take Cda = 0.85, Cdf = 0.70, petrol density 750 kg/m³ and R = 287 J/kg K, and treat the air as incompressible. Find the throat and fuel jet diameters.
   1. ṁf = 7.2 ÷ 3600 = 0.002 kg/s; ṁa = 15 × 0.002 = 0.030 kg/s.
   2. ρa = p ÷ RT = 101300 ÷ (287 × 300) = 1.177 kg/m³.
   3. Throat speed √(2Δp ÷ ρa) = √(2 × 6500 ÷ 1.177) = 105.1 m/s; At = 0.030 ÷ (0.85 × 1.177 × 105.1) = 2.854×10⁻⁴ m² = 285.4 mm², so dt = √(4 × 285.4 ÷ π) = 19.06 mm.
   4. Lift head ρfgz = 750 × 9.81 × 0.005 = 36.8 Pa, so the fuel sees 6500 − 36.8 = 6463 Pa; fuel speed √(2 × 6463 ÷ 750) = 4.152 m/s.
   5. Af = 0.002 ÷ (0.70 × 750 × 4.152) = 9.176×10⁻⁷ m² = 0.918 mm², so df = √(4 × 0.918 ÷ π) = 1.081 mm.
   6. Check: 0.030 ÷ 0.002 = 15, the required A/F.
   Answer: Throat diameter about 19.1 mm and fuel jet diameter about 1.08 mm. Neglecting the 5 mm lift gives 1.079 mm, so z matters little here. */
