/* book exam traps: The main jet alone does not set the mixture at all loads; the idle circuit, power valve, accelerator pump and choke add fuel where it falls short. | A/F is by mass, not volume. Rich means low A/F (more fuel); lean means high A/F. Students often reverse these. | Venturi depression is the pressure drop at the throat, not the manifold vacuum. At idle the closed throttle makes high manifold vacuum, but the venturi signal is weak. | In the jet formula the fuel sees Δp minus ρfgz, because it must be lifted to the nozzle tip; and 1 bar = 10⁵ Pa must be converted first. | The choke is for cold start only. Left on, it washes oil off the cylinder walls and causes rich misfire. */
