/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A fuel injection system has five jobs: meter, atomise, distribute, time and pressurise the fuel.
   - Atomisation breaks the fuel into fine droplets.
   - Distribution gives every cylinder the same fuel quantity and a spray aimed at the right place.
   - Air injection used a blast of compressed air to carry fuel into the cylinder.
   - For diesels, direct injection (DI) sprays fuel straight into a bowl in the piston crown: common rail, unit injector, in-line pump and many distributor-pump engines.
   - By control, a system is mechanical (cam, plunger and spring meter and time the fuel) or electronic (sensors, an electronic control unit, ECU, and solenoid or piezo injectors).
   - Injection timing is the crank angle at which the pump starts to deliver fuel.
   - Injection lag is the delay from the start of pump delivery to the nozzle opening.
   - Injection duration is the crank angle from nozzle opening to nozzle closing.
*/
